Nerves — sensation, muscle activation, and control (@med-nerves: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @medicine, #source @med-source-osu, #medicine @med-inflammation, #source @med-source-terminology) { r_08WV4S2F8WMCT7SZX2FHY82YP7
**Read sustained foundations:** [Anatomy & Physiology — Oregon State University](../sources/Anatomy-and-Physiology.lingua#r_1WEZWZ5YY6797K4EXVYTBJS25N), chapters 12–14 on nervous tissue, neural anatomy, and somatic sensory-motor organization, with their diagrams and review questions. Start with chapter 12 here, continue chapter 13 through [A movement system needs nerves and blood supply](../musculoskeletal/Nerves-and-blood-supply.lingua#r_0B7GSTVKPXTQPSTZVF7SPYGPK1), and apply chapter 14 as the movement readings deepen. Use [Medical Terminology 2e — Open RN](../sources/Medical-Terminology-2e.lingua#r_6R6B3VDJ3NYF8QKGJYBZTSTT3Q), chapter 16, for a language review rather than a substitute for these explanations.

A neuron/neurônio communicates through electrical and chemical processes. A peripheral nerve/nervo periférico contains many axons and supporting tissues. Sensory pathways carry information toward the central nervous system; motor pathways influence effectors such as skeletal muscle. These descriptions concern direction and function, not two visibly separate wires on every diagram.

Motor commands, sensory feedback, reflexes, attention, and learned coordination contribute to movement. Proprioception/propriocepção includes information about body position and movement. A joint's behavior therefore depends on neural control as well as its surfaces and ligaments.

A dermatome/dermátomo describes a skin territory associated with a spinal root; a named peripheral nerve supplies a different anatomical distribution. They overlap imperfectly. Sensory symptoms, weakness, and reflex findings need interpretation together.

**Try:** trace a voluntary movement from a central command through a peripheral motor axon to muscle contraction, and then trace returning sensory information. Distinguish reduced force due to pain, a muscle injury, and a nerve problem as different possibilities requiring evidence.

**Continue:** [Inflammation, infection, and repair are different processes](Inflammation-infection-and-repair.lingua#r_4CWKE5QM0EFHK2N3AP2Y6WZMX5).

**Return:** [Medicine — understanding the body and its care](../Medicine.lingua#r_6DTDCDP6JQ2AV7S78QPGKM2V65).
